2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe what temperature is measured by the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s temperature sensor<\/li>\n<li>Explain the importance of the temperature measurement<\/li>\n<li>Convert temperature measurements to different units<\/li>\n<li>Describe what a barometer is and how it can be used to measure height<\/li>\n<li>Use barometer measurements to predict the height of the drone<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Temperature Sensor<\/li>\n<li>Print the Current Temperature<\/li>\n<li>Celsius to Fahrenheit<\/li>\n<li>Barometer<\/li>\n<li>Print the Current Pressure<\/li>\n<li>More Pressure Readings<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Height Calculator Part 1<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Height Calculator Part 2<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137374964\" name=\"6a22137374964\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Temperature Sensor<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>One of the sensors onboard CoDrone EDU is the temperature sensor, which does exactly what it says: it reads the temperature. However, it does<strong> NOT<\/strong> get the temperature from its environment, so it cannot be used to tell you whether it\u2019s warm or cold where you are. What it can tell you is its internal temperature, so how cold or hot it is inside the drone.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7752&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221373749e0\" name=\"6a221373749e0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Print the Current Temperature<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Create a variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">temperature<\/code> that will store the drone\u2019s internal temperature. Attach it to the <strong><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_drone_temperature()<\/code><\/strong>block from the senior &#8220;Sensors&#8221; menu. Next, include a <strong>print<\/strong> block that will print temperature\u2019s value. Use the dropdown menu to select the units. Run the program to see how hot it is inside your drone. <em>Are you surprised by the results?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13180&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137374a59\" name=\"6a22137374a59\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Celsius to Fahrenheit<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Fun fact: most of the world uses Celsius to measure temperature, and under 10% of countries use\u00a0<a href=\"https:\/\/worldpopulationreview.com\/country-rankings\/countries-that-use-fahrenheit\">Fahrenheit<\/a>. Although the function can return units in Fahrenheit, Celsius, or Kelvin, it may be useful to do the conversion yourself. From the &#8220;Functions&#8221; menu, grab the second<strong> return function<\/strong> block and name the function &#8220;to_Fahrenheit&#8221;.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10596&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Click the gear icon to add an input. Drag the input in the <strong>inputs<\/strong> block. This function will take in a parameter of degrees in Celsius that the function will convert into degrees Fahrenheit.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10597&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The equation to convert Celsius to Fahrenheit is <code>degrees_F = degrees_C * 1.8 + 32<\/code>. Create a variable\u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">degrees_F<\/code> to store the output of the calculation. <em>Don&#8217;t forget to return the variable from the function!<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12920&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The last step is to call the function in a program. Take a temperature reading in Celsius and print the results. Then, print the converted value.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13181&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137374ab7\" name=\"6a22137374ab7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Barometer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In addition to the temperature sensor, the barometer can also measure pressure. You may have noticed that when you\u2019re in the mountains or on a plane, your ears pop! The reason this happens is due to air trapped in your inner ear. When you move up to a higher altitude, the air pressure is lower. This change causes pressure on your eardrum, which sounds and feels a lot like popping.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7754&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]CoDrone EDU has a pressure sensor, also known as a<strong> barometer<\/strong>, built in to measure the air pressure around the CoDrone EDU. That sensor can detect seemingly tiny differences in height. Barometers are often used in weather forecasting, since changes in air pressure can affect the weather. For example, a drop in atmospheric pressure means a storm is most likely on the way, while an increase in pressure usually predicts clear skies.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137374b19\" name=\"6a22137374b19\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Print the Current Pressure<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Find out what your CoDrone EDU\u2019s starting pressure is! Create a variable called\u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">pressure<\/code> that will store the drone\u2019s air pressure using the\u00a0<strong><code>get_pressure()<\/code><\/strong> function from the &#8220;Sensors&#8221; menu. The default units for pressure are in pascals, which is the international standard for air pressure measurement. Then, print the value. Try running your program while holding your drone at various heights. <em>What do you notice about the difference in air pressure?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12922&#8243; img_size=&#8221;600&#215;198&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]You can also change the units of air pressure to millibar. One millibar is 100 pascals! <em>What other units of air pressure can you think of?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12923&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137374b89\" name=\"6a22137374b89\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>More Pressure Readings<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Create a pressure variable that will get and store the air pressure at ground level. You will be creating multiple pressure variables, one for each height you want to measure the air pressure at. Create unique variables names to identify the different heights. For example, the first reading can be called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">pressure1<\/code>.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12926&#8243; img_size=&#8221;600&#215;100&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"79\">Add a takeoff to your code and take a second pressure reading. <em>Don&#8217;t forget to add the land command.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12927&#8243; img_size=&#8221;600&#215;337&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]After taking off, throttle up at 30% speed for 1 second. Create a new variable to store the value.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12928&#8243; img_size=&#8221;600&#215;664&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Finally, add a print statement at the end to see the results. <em>Are the differences in pressure large or small?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12929&#8243; img_size=&#8221;600&#215;1110&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137374c27\" name=\"6a22137374c27\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Barometer Testing<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"94\">For this challenge, you will create a relationship between height and barometer measurements. Grab a partner for this activity to take measurements more easily. Here are the steps:<\/p>\n<ul data-source-line=\"96\">\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"96\">In a forever loop, write code that will print the barometer sensor data when the space bar is pressed.<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"97\">Find a ruler or measuring tape to use for measuring the drone&#8217;s height. This is easier with a partner so that they can hold the measuring tape for you.<\/li>\n<li data-source-line=\"98\">Run your program so that it&#8217;s ready to receive key press commands.<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"99\">Move the drone with your hand to various heights. For example, measure 6 inches off the ground, 12 inches, 18 inches, etc. Press the space bar at each reading and record the result.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"101\">Finally, analyze the results. <em>How sensitive is the barometer? Can you figure out a relationship between the pressure and height? In other words, what is the difference in pascals for every 6 inches?<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137374d10\" name=\"6a22137374d10\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about the CoDrone EDU\u2019s temperature sensor and barometer. The temperature sensor and barometer can help you observe important info about the CoDrone EDU\u2019s environment and status! These sensors are also used to estimate height and altitude.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7547&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 We've Moved! [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1761251882568{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: Robolink Learn! Lessons here&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-3-temperature-and-air-pressure-cde-blockly\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4742,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions"